## IN-CONTEXT DRAFT SHAPE (no file — LLM context only)

The agent carries the following dict-shaped draft in its conversation context — no file written. A Phase 0 `<thinking>` block should establish this shape; subsequent phases update it in-memory:

```
{
  mode: "A" | "B",
  inferences: {
    source:        "<.eml path / pasted text / screenshot description / null>",
    confidence:    "HIGH" | "MEDIUM" | "LOW",
    extracted:     { subject, sender, status, sections, metrics, team_labels, workstream_names },
    missing:       [ ... field names ... ]
  },
  project:       { name, region, date: <today>, sender, ccList },
  status:        { overallDelivery: {color, rationale}, gdcProject: {color, rationale} },
  sections:      { summary, completed, inProgress, ask, attention },   // bullets + evidenceMap per bullet
  inputs:        { leaves, weeksElapsed, jiraPaste, freeformUpdates },  // from Phase 1 reply
  metrics:       { sprint, capacityUtilisation, userStoryDelivered, defectsPerDays (per-phase) },
  gaps:          [ ... "⚠️ MISSING — ..." strings ... ],
  validationReport: { ... from silent Phase 2 run ... },
  sycophancyNudgesShown: [ ... ]
}
```

**Persistence policy:**
- This draft lives ONLY in the LLM context window during the run. No file writes.
- The **only** on-disk artifacts are the 4 bundle files written at SP2 (one-shot, post-approval):
  `subject.txt`, `email_body.html`, `validation_report.md`, `run_metadata.json`.
- `run_metadata.json` is a **post-hoc audit snapshot** of the run (mode, inferences, inputs, gaps, timestamp) — not runtime state.

> **Why stateless?** A WSR run is a short, single-session flow (3 phases, 2 stops, completes in minutes). The LLM's 200k context window comfortably holds reference + inferences + batch reply + draft + validation without needing file persistence. Resume across sessions is not required for this use case.

